WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.640
When you're traveling on a train&nbsp;
through the middle of nowhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:10.560
chances are your internet is spotty or just plain&nbsp;
disappears. What if your connection is not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:19.360
stable but it's just as good as it is in a big&nbsp;
city? Ever since 2019, SpaceX has been launching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:24.480
batches of about 60 Starlink satellites at a&nbsp;
time. You can see the Starlink satellites gently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:30.240
floating away. Enough are now in orbit to start&nbsp;
its plan to connect nearly every inch of the world

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.840
A Native American tribe that lives in a remote&nbsp;
part of Washington State is one of the first to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:47.200
try out the new internet. Before the Hoh Tribe&nbsp;
got Starlink as part of a private beta test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:52.480
their internet wasn't great and that's putting&nbsp;
it lightly. They've had to deal with astonishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:01:00.160
rates of 0.3 to 0.7 megabits per second. They&nbsp;
can't even stream a YouTube video in standard 480p&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.880
which requires at least a megabit a second.&nbsp;
The global average for broadband connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:12.560
is 84 megabits per second. SpaceX just came up&nbsp;
and just catapulted us into the 21st century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:18.560
Our youth are able to do education online.&nbsp;
Initial tests of Starlink show it can download&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:24.400
greater than 100 megabits a second according to&nbsp;
SpaceX. Another advantage is supposed to be low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.760
latency which measures delay. For example, the&nbsp;
time it takes between clicking on a web page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.520
and when the page is displayed. SpaceX says the&nbsp;
lag is around 20 milliseconds - good enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.880
play the fastest games online and on par with&nbsp;
ground-based services like fiber-optic broadband.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.160
Low latency is achieved because the satellites&nbsp;
are much closer to Earth, with most sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:53.840
550 km or 340 miles above the planet's surface&nbsp;
whereas many others are at least 1,000 km out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.600
But there are disadvantages to sitting at a lower&nbsp;
altitude. The satellites will see less of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:06.560
planet so a lot more are needed to cover an area.&nbsp;
That's why SpaceX plans to put 12,000 into orbit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:12.880
and possibly as many as 42,000 one day.&nbsp;
Considering only 9,000 satellites have ever been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:19.200
launched in all of history, this is astounding and&nbsp;
not going unnoticed when you look up at the sky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.840
The latest ones have a visor to make them less&nbsp;
shiny than these but astronomers doubt this will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:29.600
make them completely invisible to the eye. Besides&nbsp;
the impact on observations, questions have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.520
raised about how this will add to debris in space&nbsp;
commonly called space junk, defined as any object&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:41.760
that no longer has a purpose. SpaceX had to show&nbsp;
the FCC it has a plan to address the debris issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:47.520
before getting approval to launch in the US. The&nbsp;
company says satellites that fail or are old will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.080
intentionally deorbit with their thrusters&nbsp;
and burn up in the atmosphere so they won't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:58.320
become debris. A benefit of flying low is rapid&nbsp;
re-entry. Avoiding collisions is another pressing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.600
matter. The satellites are equipped with tracking&nbsp;
technology from the U.S. military to dodge debris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.800
or spacecraft - although a European satellite did&nbsp;
have to maneuver out of the way of a Starlink last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.520
year. SpaceX then fixed a computer glitch it&nbsp;
said led to a communication breakdown with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:19.440
European Space Agency. These are concerns&nbsp;
SpaceX is facing as it launches rapidly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.240
Over 700 satellites are now in orbit enough&nbsp;
to start rolling at public beta tests in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:29.600
Northern U.S. and in southern Canada. Users will&nbsp;
connect to the constellation with a terminal the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:35.120
size of a small or medium pizza. Apparently, it&nbsp;
doesn't require special expertise to install.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:39.760
You plug it in and aim it to get a clear view&nbsp;
of the sky. While it might be easy to use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.920
the difficulty may be getting your hands on&nbsp;
the device because governments could ban them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:50.480
In my previous video about Starlink, I talked&nbsp;
about internet censorship in countries like China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.680
and what would happen if SpaceX gave citizens&nbsp;
unfiltered internet access without permission.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.960
Here's what Elon Musk once said. If they get&nbsp;
they get upset with us, they can blow our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:06.400
satellites up, which wouldn't be good. China can&nbsp;
do that, so probably we shouldn't broadcast there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:13.040
Another market Starlink is unlikely to touch&nbsp;
is high-density areas because it doesn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.080
sufficient bandwidth - meaning the volume of&nbsp;
information it can handle at a given time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:22.880
But that doesn't mean those living in cities&nbsp;
won't be impacted. Musk has confirmed Starlink&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.760
can work on moving objects like trains and&nbsp;
people can potentially use it no matter where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:33.120
they are at sea. SpaceX wants to test it out on&nbsp;
its drone ships used to help land the boosters of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:39.200
the Falcon 9 rockets. There is a more substantial&nbsp;
impact for people who already have good internet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:43.680
As more of the world comes online, this&nbsp;
will change the demographics of the web,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.200
the languages that dominate, where&nbsp;
the advertising dollars flow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:53.840
what businesses emerge to cater to new users. 40%&nbsp;
of the population doesn't have internet access.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:59.360
If billions of people come online, the world&nbsp;
wide web will look like a very different place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:05.200
And it could happen very soon. SpaceX wants&nbsp;
to roll at coverage globally by 2021 or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:11.760
2022. As life-changing as it could be, a global&nbsp;
internet is actually a step toward a bigger goal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:18.560
That goal is building a city on Mars. SpaceX is&nbsp;
banking on Starlink to eventually bring in 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:23.520
billion dollars a year - 10 times more than what&nbsp;
it makes launching payloads to the International&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:29.520
Space Station. We don't yet know how much Starlink&nbsp;
will cost but SpaceX's president and COO Gwynne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:35.120
Shotwell did point out in an interview million of&nbsp;
people in the U.S. pay 80 bucks a month for crappy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:41.280
service, in her words. SpaceX claims hundreds&nbsp;
of thousands of people have already expressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:46.640
interest. If starlink fulfills its promise of&nbsp;
connecting the most remote locations on earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:51.440
if it replaces a poor connection with a&nbsp;
good one, if it's relatively affordable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:56.480
then a massive disruption is about to happen.&nbsp;
Hey guys, I hope you enjoyed the story. Thanks so&nbsp;&nbsp;

